at  as the price of getting the memoirs out  he was forced to put in some  disinformation that would throw off  western intelligence  my take on them now is that he told the  truth as he knew it but khrushchev's  view in many cases was very distorted he  also didn't have access to his own  records your next book  my next book is on bush and gorbachev  which i am writing in collaboration with  a journalist strobe talbot of time  magazine we are aspiring to  write an account of how this  relationship has developed between these  two leaders  between 1989 and 1992 based on  interviews with soviets and americans  were expecting it to come out in the  fall of 1992. and last question are you  happy with the way this book's played  out and the way it's being  bought and read  i have  this is a book that in many ways very  much disagrees with  much of what has been written about the  history of the crisis years over the  last 30 years  many of the people who have reviewed it  have been people whose work i have to  some extent revised based on both new  information and also additional  perspective  and they've treated it respectfully and  for that i'm grateful michael bashlas  author of the crisis years  kennedy and khrushchev  1960 to 1963. thank you for joining us  thank you brian  michael beschloss is the author of  several books on the american presidency  he's also a regular commentator on the  pbs program the newshour with jim lehrer  his book the crisis years kennedy and  khrushchev was published in 1991 by  harper trade  mr besh loss is one of the six  contributors to power in the presidency  published by public affairs books  every weekend book tv brings you an  encore presentation of c-span's book  notes program encore book notes airs  saturdays at 7 p.m and sundays at 11 a.m  eastern here on c-span 2.  next week an encore book notes interview  with former soviet leader mikhail  gorbachev his book memoirs recounts his  career as a communist party leader  you can fi